#edb/ref 17592186434435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434445 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89c151cf-1aa4-5332-ba88-97da2a96de62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $R:$\n\n- $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}\\le 9$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $5x-3>7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28a2abe2-d607-5ca5-a1d6-25d691cd727c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434446 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have that\n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- To find the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le 9 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is \n${T}_{P}=[-3,3].$ \nAs a result, the complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- To find the truth set of $Q(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & >7 \\\\ 5x & >10 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $Q(x)$ is\n${T}_{Q}=(2,\\infty ),$\nand its complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}=(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =[-3,3]\\cap (2,\\infty ) \\\\  & =(2,3] \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =((-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ))\\cap (-\\infty ,2] \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-3) \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =(2,3]\\cup (-\\infty ,-3) \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (2,3].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/10/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d9ce0aa-3342-547e-8dda-214837653ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434447 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}:$ \n\n![](images/f0/f02e2138f843c5a350c8be6c81a408c4af51fb9b19ced3fa26bfc466493008ac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x)∨Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{p}$ or ${T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/45/45e38853893ceec19f969a1611cd50bbb9ff97e7bfd617c6e2e0a6268fb4b8fa.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dd/dde2aacce3d0707ddb09c06ca305645eadba1aedca9858eacc24abd46d09b03c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
